# VoIPmonitor C++ Network Packet Sniffer - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is VoIPmonitor, a high-performance C++ network packet sniffer for VoIP protocols (SIP, RTP, RTCP, SKINNY/SCCP). It's designed for real-time network analysis, call quality monitoring, and telecommunications data processing.

## Code Style & Standards

### C++ Standards
- Use C++11/14 features where appropriate
- Follow RAII principles for resource management
- Prefer stack allocation over heap when possible
- Use smart pointers for dynamic memory management
- Maintain thread safety in multi-threaded contexts

### Naming Conventions
- Classes: PascalCase (e.g., `CallTable`, `PcapQueue`)
- Functions/methods: camelCase or snake_case (follow existing patterns)
- Variables: snake_case for local variables, camelCase for members
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- File names: snake_case.cpp/.h

### Header Files
- Always use include guards (`#ifndef HEADER_H` / `#define HEADER_H` / `#endif`)
- Forward declarations in headers when possible
- Include order: system headers, then project headers
- Separate interface (.h) from implementation (.cpp)

## Architecture Patterns

### Memory Management
- Use RAII for automatic resource cleanup
- Implement proper copy constructors and assignment operators
- Consider move semantics for performance-critical code
- Use memory pools for high-frequency allocations
- Be mindful of memory leaks in long-running network services

### Threading & Concurrency
- Use pthread primitives or C++11 threading facilities
- Implement proper synchronization with mutexes/locks
- Consider lock-free data structures for performance
- Use atomic operations for shared counters/flags
- Implement proper cleanup in thread termination

### Network Programming
- Handle endianness conversion properly
- Validate all network input data
- Implement proper error handling for socket operations
- Use non-blocking I/O where appropriate
- Consider packet processing pipeline architecture

## Performance Considerations

### High-Performance Network Processing
- Minimize memory allocations in packet processing paths
- Use efficient data structures (vectors over lists for cache locality)
- Consider SIMD instructions for bulk data operations
- Implement zero-copy techniques where possible
- Profile and optimize hot paths

### Database Operations
- Use prepared statements for MySQL operations
- Implement connection pooling
- Batch database operations when possible
- Handle database reconnection gracefully
- Consider asynchronous database writes

## Error Handling

### Network Errors
- Handle partial reads/writes properly
- Implement timeout mechanisms
- Gracefully handle network disconnections
- Log network errors with appropriate detail
- Implement retry logic with exponential backoff

### System Errors
- Check return values of all system calls
- Use errno for system error reporting
- Implement proper signal handling
- Handle resource exhaustion gracefully
- Provide meaningful error messages

## Build System & Dependencies

### Autotools Integration
- Maintain configure.in for dependency detection
- Update Makefile.in for new source files
- Handle conditional compilation for optional features
- Ensure cross-platform compatibility (Linux/FreeBSD)

### Dependencies
- MySQL/MariaDB client libraries
- libpcap for packet capture
- OpenSSL for cryptographic operations
- Various audio codec libraries
- RRD tools for statistics
- JSON libraries for configuration

## Security Considerations

### Network Security
- Validate all packet data before processing
- Implement bounds checking for buffer operations
- Handle malformed packets gracefully
- Sanitize data before database insertion
- Implement proper SSL/TLS handling

### Privilege Management
- Drop privileges after initialization when possible
- Validate file permissions before access
- Implement secure temporary file handling
- Audit sensitive operations

## Testing & Debugging

### Unit Testing
- Create tests for packet parsing functions
- Test edge cases and malformed input
- Verify thread safety of concurrent operations
- Test database operations with mock data

### Debugging
- Use appropriate logging levels (debug, info, warning, error)
- Implement structured logging for network events
- Use debugging symbols in development builds
- Consider memory debugging tools (valgrind, AddressSanitizer)

## Documentation

### Code Documentation
- Document complex algorithms and data structures
- Explain protocol-specific implementations
- Document thread safety guarantees
- Include performance considerations in comments

### Configuration
- Document all configuration parameters
- Provide examples for common use cases
- Explain performance implications of settings
- Document network requirements and firewall rules

## File Organization

### Source Structure
- Keep related functionality in dedicated files
- Separate protocol handlers into individual modules
- Maintain clear separation between network and database layers
- Group utility functions in tools.cpp/tools.h

### Headers
- Use forward declarations to reduce compilation dependencies
- Keep public interfaces clean and minimal
- Separate internal implementation details
- Maintain consistent include patterns

## Specific VoIPmonitor Patterns

### Packet Processing
- Follow the existing packet processing pipeline
- Implement proper RTP/RTCP sequence handling
- Handle SIP message parsing correctly
- Maintain call state consistency

### Call Management
- Use the CallTable pattern for call tracking
- Implement proper call lifecycle management
- Handle call transfers and redirections
- Maintain accurate call statistics

### Configuration Management
- Use the existing config_param system
- Implement hot-reload capabilities where appropriate
- Validate configuration parameters
- Provide sensible defaults

## Common Pitfalls to Avoid

- Don't block the main packet processing thread
- Avoid excessive memory allocations in hot paths
- Don't ignore return values from system calls
- Avoid hardcoded buffer sizes for network data
- Don't assume network byte order matches host order
- Avoid race conditions in multi-threaded code
- Don't leak file descriptors or network sockets

## When Adding New Features

1. Consider impact on packet processing performance
2. Ensure thread safety in multi-threaded contexts
3. Add appropriate configuration parameters
4. Implement proper error handling and logging
5. Update relevant documentation
6. Consider backward compatibility
7. Test with various network conditions and packet types